# 13. Rendering architecture

The app should use a deterministic renderer.

Good rendering options:

| Option                   | Pros                                         | Cons                               |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| HTML/CSS renderer        | Fast to build, familiar, easy text rendering | Print-perfect layout can be tricky |
| SVG renderer             | Precise, exportable, good for overlays       | More custom layout code            |
| Canvas renderer          | Flexible visual rendering                    | Text/layout editing can be harder  |
| Hybrid HTML/SVG          | Good balance                                 | More complexity                    |
| Server-side PDF renderer | Great for print export                       | Requires backend/export pipeline   |

Recommended approach:

**Use React + TypeScript for editor, generate an internal layout JSON, render to SVG/HTML for preview, and export via Playwright or Sharp.**

For MVP:

* Frontend: React or Next.js
* Editor state: Zustand or Redux Toolkit
* Canvas interactions: Konva.js, Fabric.js, or custom SVG selection layer
* Rendering: SVG or HTML/CSS
* Export: Playwright screenshot for PNG/PDF, or SVG-to-PNG pipeline
* Storage: SQLite with Prisma, or local IndexedDB for a local-first prototype
* Image processing: Sharp on backend
* File packaging: ZIP export

For a local/self-hosted version:

* Docker Compose
* Web app container
* SQLite/Postgres volume
* Asset storage volume
* Optional worker for exports

